      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine the sign of the fraction on the left-hand side of the inequality. We will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the numerator and the denominator:**\n   - The numerator is \\(f(x) = x + 6\\).\n   - The denominator is \\(g(x) = x^2 + 2x + 7\\).\n\n2. **Analyze the numerator:**\n   - The numerator \\(f(x) = x + 6\\) is a linear function.\n   - It is always positive because \\(x + 6 > 0\\) for all \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\) (since \\(x \\neq -6\\)).\n\n3. **Analyze the denominator:**\n   - The denominator \\(g(x) = x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is a quadratic function.\n   - To determine if it is always positive, we can analyze its discriminant.\n     \\[\n     \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\n     \\]\n   - Since the discriminant is negative (\\(\\Delta < 0\\)), the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots and is always positive for all real \\(x\\).\n\n4. **Combine the results:**\n   - The fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) will be positive when both the numerator and the denominator are positive or both are negative.\n   - Since the numerator \\(x + 6\\) is always positive, the fraction will be positive when the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is also positive.\n\n5. **Determine the sign of the denominator:**\n   - As established, \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive regardless of the value of \\(x\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since both the numerator and the denominator are always positive, the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is always positive for all real \\(x\\).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, \\infty)}\n\\]",
