      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine the values of \\(x\\) for which the fraction is non-negative. The fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is non-negative if both the numerator and the denominator are either both non-negative or both non-positive.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the numerator \\(x + 6\\). The numerator is zero when \\(x = -6\\). The numerator is positive when \\(x > -6\\) and negative when \\(x < -6\\).\n\nNext, let's analyze the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). The quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) can be rewritten by completing the square:\n\\[x^2 + 2x + 7 = (x + 1)^2 + 6.\\]\nSince \\((x + 1)^2 \\ge 0\\) for all real \\(x\\), it follows that \\((x + 1)^2 + 6 \\ge 6\\) for all real \\(x\\). Therefore, the denominator is always positive and never zero.\n\nGiven that the denominator is always positive, the sign of the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) depends solely on the sign of the numerator \\(x + 6\\). Thus, the fraction is non-negative if and only if \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\), which simplifies to \\(x \\ge -6\\).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
